Q: Angular TypeScript Element implicitly has an &#39;any&#39; type because expression of type &#39;1&#39; can&#39;t be used to index A: <p>Note that you are not setting the returned response to the <code>results</code> signal. You should set the <code>data</code> instead of empty <code>DataLayer</code> object to the signal.</p>
<pre class="lang-ts prettyprint-override"><code>this.results.set(data);
</code></pre>
<p>Next, you need to modify the type for <code>results</code> signal to match the value. You should have these interfaces/types:</p>
<pre class="lang-ts prettyprint-override"><code>interface DataLayer {
    name: string;
    capital: string;
    region: string;
    income: string;
    long: string;
    lat: string;
}

interface PaginationResult {
    page: number,
    pages: number,
    per_page: string,
    total: number
}

type PaginatedDataLayer = [
    PaginationResult,
    DataLayer[]
]
</code></pre>
<pre class="lang-ts prettyprint-override"><code>results: signal&lt;PaginatedDataLayer | undefined&gt;(undefined);
</code></pre>

Q: Use named parameters when calling a Powershell class method A: <p>A <em>really</em> simple way would be to just initialise variables with the parameter names:</p>
<pre><code>class A {
    static method ($a, $b) {
        Write-Host &quot;$a $b&quot;
    }
}

$a = “a”
$b = “b”

[A]::method($a, $b)
</code></pre>
<p>It might get a bit verbose if you have hundreds of call sites, but it <em>looks</em> pretty close to named parameters if you squint.</p>
<p>Another option would be to wrap the class method in a cmdlet purely to provide the named parameter aesthetic (although this will probably still suffer the “parameter binding” perfotmance problem of the original question as posted, but it <em>does</em> at least let you call a class method with named parameters in a roundabout way):</p>
<pre><code>class A {
    static method ($a, $b) {
        Write-Host &quot;$a $b&quot;
    }
}

function MyMethod
{
    param( $a, $b )
    [A]::method($a, $b)
}

MyMethod -a “a” -b “b”
</code></pre>
<p>Neither method is perfect, and you’ll need to measure performance, but both approaches let you keep the original signature of the class method with all the type safety that brings.</p>

Q: Controlling swipe inputs in unity A: <p>It seems like you never initialised <code>desiredPosition</code> in your code.</p>

<p>I did not successfully reproduce your issue, but theoretically I believe this should work. Please let us know if it helped.</p>

<p>Assuming no other forces act on the <code>Player</code> tranform (it won't be moved other than by this script):</p>

<pre><code>public class swipeTest : MonoBehaviour {

    private void Start() {
        desiredPosition = Player.position;
    }
}
</code></pre>

<p>Or if other forces act on <code>Player</code> you should update it every time:</p>

<pre><code>public class swipeTest : MonoBehaviour {


    private void Update() {

    desiredPosition = Player.position;

        if (swipeControls.SwipeLeft)
            desiredPosition += Vector3.left;
        if (swipeControls.SwipeRight)
            desiredPosition += Vector3.right;

        Player.transform.position = Vector3.MoveTowards
            (Player.transform.position, desiredPosition, 0.5f * Time.deltaTime);
    }
}
</code></pre>

Q: Different results between nslookup and Java&#39;s InetAddress getHostName A: <p>It is due to the "A" record check - Java wants that IP number you're looking up, was listed there. They call it "XXX" and I understand why:</p>

<pre><code>private static String getHostFromNameService(InetAddress addr, boolean check) {
String host = null;
for (NameService nameService : nameServices) {
    try {
        // first lookup the hostname
        host = nameService.getHostByAddr(addr.getAddress());

        /* check to see if calling code is allowed to know
         * the hostname for this IP address, ie, connect to the host
         */
        if (check) {
            SecurityManager sec = System.getSecurityManager();
            if (sec != null) {
                sec.checkConnect(host, -1);
            }
        }

        /* now get all the IP addresses for this hostname,
         * and make sure one of them matches the original IP
         * address. We do this to try and prevent spoofing.
         */

        InetAddress[] arr = InetAddress.getAllByName0(host, check);
        boolean ok = false;

        if(arr != null) {
            for(int i = 0; !ok &amp;&amp; i &lt; arr.length; i++) {
                ok = addr.equals(arr[i]);
            }
        }

        //XXX: if it looks a spoof just return the address?
        if (!ok) {
            host = addr.getHostAddress();
            return host;
        }

        break;
</code></pre>
